rows.body:
`MultiSelectListFilterContent` subscribes to the full `ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY` and `ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY_CATEGORIES` collections with no selector, yet the values are only used to compute three booleans (`shouldShowTag`, `shouldShowCategory`, `shouldShowSubmittedViolation`) inside `getHasOptionAvailability`. Without a selector, Onyx uses `shallowEqual` on the raw collection references, so this component re-renders on every write to any policy or any policy category (both hot collections), even though the derived output is unchanged. This is precisely the "compute a final scalar from a larger dataset" case that should use a `selector`.

Move the reduction into a `selector` that returns the small scalar object so `deepEqual` runs on three booleans instead of the whole collection, e.g.:

```tsx
const hasFilter = baseFilterKey === CONST.SEARCH.SYNTAX_FILTER_KEYS.HAS;
const [policyCategories] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY_CATEGORIES);
const [hasOptionAvailability] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY, {
    selector: (policies) => (hasFilter ? getHasOptionAvailability(policies, policyCategories) : undefined),
});
```

(exposing `getHasOptionAvailability` and threading its result through so the component only re-renders when one of the derived booleans actually changes).

This subscribes to the entire `NVP_LOCKED_VBA_UNLOCK_REQUESTED` collection, so `WalletPage` re-renders whenever any bank account's unlock marker changes, even ones unrelated to the account being viewed. The other three call sites in this PR correctly subscribe to just the single relevant collection key.

Subscribe to the specific key instead of the whole collection:

```tsx
// eslint-disable-next-line rulesdir/no-default-id-values
const [unlockRequestedAt] = useOnyx(`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.NVP_LOCKED_VBA_UNLOCK_REQUESTED}${accountData?.bankAccountID ?? CONST.DEFAULT_NUMBER_ID}`);
```

The `selector` passed to `usePersonalDetailsByLogins` builds a brand-new array on every store read (`adminEmails.map(...)`, `[...adminsWithInfo]`, and the `tokenizedSearch` result). This hook is backed by `useSyncExternalStore`, and its own JSDoc explicitly states the selector "must return a primitive or a reference that is already stable... never a freshly built object or array." Returning a fresh array defeats change detection: React sees a new reference on every read and re-renders the component on every store update (and can warn "The result of getSnapshot should be cached").

Subscribe without a selector to get the stable, reference-cached map, then build `adminsList` in the component body (the React Compiler memoizes it):

```tsx
const adminEmails = admins?.filter((admin) => admin !== currentUserPersonalDetails?.email) ?? [];
const personalDetailsByLogin = usePersonalDetailsByLogins(adminEmails);
const adminsWithInfo = adminEmails.map((admin) => {
    const personalDetails = personalDetailsByLogin[admin];
    const formattedAdmin = formatMemberForList({ /* ... */ });
    return {...formattedAdmin, isInteractive: false};
});
let adminsList = adminsWithInfo;
if (debouncedSearchTerm) {
    const searchValue = getSearchValueForPhoneOrEmail(debouncedSearchTerm, countryCode).toLowerCase();
    adminsList = tokenizedSearch(adminsWithInfo, searchValue, (option) => [option.text ?? '', option.alternateText ?? '']);
}
```

The `selector` passed to `usePersonalDetailsByLogins` builds a brand-new array on every store read via `adminEmails.flatMap(...)`. This hook is backed by `useSyncExternalStore`, and its own JSDoc explicitly states the selector "must return a primitive or a reference that is already stable (e.g. a field of the personal details), never a freshly built object or array." Returning a fresh array makes React see a new reference on every read, so the change-detection benefit is lost and the component re-renders on every store update (React can also warn "The result of getSnapshot should be cached").

Use the selector only to narrow to the stable slice you need (or subscribe without a selector) and build the `MemberForList[]` array outside the hook, in the component body, where the React Compiler memoizes it:

```tsx
const adminEmails = getEligibleBankAccountShareRecipientEmails(allPolicies, currentUserLogin, bankAccountID);
const personalDetailsByLogin = usePersonalDetailsByLogins(adminEmails);
const admins = adminEmails.flatMap((email) => {
    const personalDetails = personalDetailsByLogin[email];
    if (!personalDetails) {
        return [];
    }
    return [formatMemberForList({
        text: personalDetails.displayName,
        alternateText: personalDetails.login,
        keyForList: personalDetails.login ?? String(personalDetails.accountID),
        accountID: personalDetails.accountID,
        login: personalDetails.login,
        pendingAction: personalDetails.pendingAction,
        reportID: '',
    })];
});
```

This subscribes to the entire `NVP_ONBOARDING` object with no selector, so the component re-renders whenever any field of that NVP changes, even though the only value read from `onboarding` is `hasCompletedGuidedSetupFlow` (used once for `isOnboardingCompleted`). This is also a redundant second subscription to the same key that `guidedSetupAndTourStatus` (line 76) already reads via a selector.

Drop the extra broad subscription and reuse the selector result you already have, or add a selector that returns just the boolean:

```tsx
// hasCompletedGuidedSetupFlow is already derived on line 79 from guidedSetupAndTourStatus
const isOnboardingCompleted = hasCompletedGuidedSetupFlow ?? false;
```

If the exact raw value (without the selector's empty-object fallback) is required, use a scalar selector instead:

```tsx
const [isOnboardingCompleted] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.NVP_ONBOARDING, {selector: (onboarding) => onboarding?.hasCompletedGuidedSetupFlow ?? false});
```

This subscribes `InboxTabSelector` to the entire `REPORT_NAME_VALUE_PAIRS` collection with no selector, but the value is never rendered — it is only read inside `confirmMarkAllAsRead` and forwarded to `markAllMessagesAsRead`. As written, this sidebar component will re-render whenever any report's name-value pairs change anywhere in the app, even though none of that data drives its UI.

Since the data is only needed imperatively at confirmation time, don't subscribe in the render path. Read it inside the action instead, the same way `markAllMessagesAsRead` already reads `REPORT` and `REPORT_ACTIONS` via `Onyx.connectWithoutView`:

```ts
// In MarkAllMessageAsRead.tsx
let allReportNameValuePairs: OnyxCollection<ReportNameValuePairs>;
Onyx.connectWithoutView({
    key: ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT_NAME_VALUE_PAIRS,
    callback: (value) => (allReportNameValuePairs = value),
});

function markAllMessagesAsRead() {
    // ...use allReportNameValuePairs directly...
}
```

Then drop the `useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT_NAME_VALUE_PAIRS)` subscription and the `reportNameValuePairs` argument from the component so the sidebar no longer re-renders on unrelated RNVP updates.

This subscribes to the entire `POLICY` collection with no `selector`, then reduces it to a single boolean (`hasHomeAndOfficeWorkspace`). Without a selector, this hook (used in both `TimeSensitiveSection` and `ProfilePage`) re-renders whenever *any* field of *any* policy changes, even though it only cares about one derived boolean.

Push the reduction into a selector so Onyx only re-renders when the boolean actually flips:

```ts
const [hasHomeAndOfficeWorkspace] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY, {
    selector: (policies) =>
        Object.values(policies ?? {}).some(
            (policy) =>
                policy?.commuterExclusions?.method === CONST.POLICY.COMMUTER_EXCLUSION_METHOD.HOME_AND_OFFICE &&
                policy?.pendingAction !== CONST.RED_BRICK_ROAD_PENDING_ACTION.DELETE,
        ),
});
```

This new subscription reads the entire `REPORT_ACTIONS` collection for the report without a `selector`. Onyx will only `shallowEqual` the raw reference, so the hook re-runs whenever any report action on that report changes, even though the value is only used to derive a single boolean (whether the report was forwarded since the last submit) downstream in `canEditMoneyRequest` -> `hasReportBeenForwardedSinceLastSubmit`. Note the sibling `reportAction` subscription in this same file already narrows via a `selector`.

Consider reducing the subscription to the boolean the caller actually needs so unrelated report-action updates don't re-run this hook, for example:

```ts
const [hasBeenForwardedSinceLastSubmit] = useOnyx(`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT_ACTIONS}${getNonEmptyStringOnyxID(transaction?.reportID)}`, {
    selector: (reportActions) => hasReportBeenForwardedSinceLastSubmit(iouReport, reportActions),
});
```

and pass that boolean into `canEditMoneyRequest` instead of the full collection. If the full collection is genuinely required for other reasons, keep it, but confirm the broad subscription is intentional.

The new `privateIsArchivedSelector` maps the entire `REPORT_NAME_VALUE_PAIRS` collection into a same-sized `Record<string, boolean>` (one entry per report). This is the "filter/map an entire collection" case PERF-11 flags: the output is still large, so `deepEqual` runs over every entry on each collection update while providing no re-render narrowing benefit. The prior approach of subscribing without a selector and building the map inline used the cheaper `shallowEqual` reference check and should be kept.

Drop the selector and build the map in the hook body (or index into the raw collection on demand inside `createOptionList`):

```tsx
const [reportNameValuePairs, reportNameValuePairsMetadata] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT_NAME_VALUE_PAIRS);
// derive privateIsArchived inline where needed instead of in the selector
```

The new `reportAttributesSelector` maps the entire `reportAttributes.reports` collection into a same-sized `Record<string, Pick<ReportAttributes, 'reportErrors' | 'brickRoadStatus'>>` — one entry per report. With a selector, Onyx runs `deepEqual` over this large output on *every* `REPORT_ATTRIBUTES` update, and since the output isn't narrowed to a small value there are no re-render savings to offset that cost. PERF-11 explicitly flags "Selector maps an entire collection — deepEqual must compare every item." The previous code (subscribe without a selector, then transform inline) was the correct pattern here because `shallowEqual` on the raw reference is far cheaper.

Subscribe without a selector and reshape inline in the hook body instead:

```tsx
const [reportAttributes, reportAttributesMetadata] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.DERIVED.REPORT_ATTRIBUTES);
// ...then pick the fields you need inline (or inside createOptionList) rather than in the selector
```

This selector filters the entire `TRANSACTION` collection (described in the code as holding "tens of thousands of entries") into a `Transaction[]` of full transaction objects. When a `useOnyx` subscription uses a `selector`, Onyx runs `deepEqual` on the selector output on every collection update to decide whether to re-render. Because the output is a large array of full `Transaction` objects, that `deepEqual` is expensive and runs on every unrelated change to the collection — this is exactly the "selector filters/maps a collection into an array" anti-pattern, made worse by returning whole objects rather than a narrowed result. It does not actually save the per-render scan the comment claims, since the scan cost simply moves to per-update.

Downstream the result is only used for its IDs (`pendingAddTransactions.map((transaction) => transaction.transactionID)`). Since `localTransactions` is already subscribed to (by key), drop the selector and derive the pending-add IDs inline inside the existing `useMemo`, as the previous code did:

```ts
const [localTransactions] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.TRANSACTION);
// ...inside the useMemo:
const pendingAddIDs = Object.values(localTransactions ?? {})
    .filter((transaction) => transaction?.pendingAction === CONST.RED_BRICK_ROAD_PENDING_ACTION.ADD)
    .map((transaction) => transaction?.transactionID);
```

This keeps the cheap `shallowEqual` on the raw `localTransactions` reference and avoids a redundant second subscription plus the costly `deepEqual` on a large array output.

This subscribes to the entire `SESSION` object but only reads `accountID`. Without a selector, Onyx compares the whole session reference, so any unrelated session field change will re-trigger this hook and recompute the expensive memoized `createFilteredOptionList` (which now lists `currentUserAccountID` as a dependency). Narrow the subscription to the primitive `accountID` using the existing shared selector.

```tsx
import {accountIDSelector} from '@selectors/Session';
// ...
const [currentUserAccountID = CONST.DEFAULT_NUMBER_ID] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.SESSION, {selector: accountIDSelector});
```

This subscribes each rendered expense-report row to the *entire* `TRANSACTION_VIOLATIONS` collection with no selector. Because there is no narrowing, Onyx falls back to `shallowEqual` on the whole collection and every row re-renders whenever *any* transaction's violations change anywhere in the app — not just this row's. The value is only consumed inside `handleActionButtonPress` (on button press), so keeping the full collection live-subscribed in render is pure overhead that scales with list size.

Instead of live-subscribing to the whole collection per row, read the specific transactions' violations you need (e.g. via a targeted selector keyed by this report's transaction IDs), or resolve `allViolations` lazily at press time inside the handler rather than subscribing in the row body. For example, narrow to just the reportItem's transactions:

```tsx
const [allViolations] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.TRANSACTION_VIOLATIONS, {
    selector: (violations) => getViolationsForReportTransactions(violations, reportItem.transactions),
});
```

so `deepEqual` runs only over this row's relevant violations and unrelated updates no longer re-render the row.

This `useOnyx` selector on the `REPORT` collection returns `projectYourSpendReports(...)`, whose `statusByID` field is a `Record<string, number>` that grows with the number of the user's paid-group reports. Onyx runs `deepEqual` on this output on *every* `REPORT` collection change (a very hot key), and the result is an intermediate structure that `getYourSpendPendingBuckets` only reduces further. On a high-traffic account this is an expensive `deepEqual` with no re-render savings — exactly the anti-pattern this rule warns about.

Mirror the sibling `outstandingReportsSignature` selector above, which reduces to a compact primitive. Compute the final booleans in the selector (or a compact string signature) rather than returning a growing `statusByID` map, e.g.:

```ts
const [reportsProjection] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT, {
    // return a small, stable signature/booleans instead of a per-report Record
    selector: (reports) => projectYourSpendReportsSignature(reports, paidGroupPolicyIDs, accountID),
});
```

`waitingReportIDsSelector` filters and maps the entire `ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT` collection into a `string[]`. When a `useOnyx` call uses a `selector`, Onyx runs `deepEqual` on the selector output to decide whether to re-render. Returning an array whose size scales with the number of matching reports forces an expensive `deepEqual` over every element on every REPORT-collection update, with no re-render savings — exactly the "selector filters/maps a collection into an array" anti-pattern.

Subscribe to the collection without a selector (so Onyx uses the cheap `shallowEqual`) and derive the waiting report IDs inline, or fold the membership check directly into the final boolean already computed by `shouldShowAddBankAccountSelector` so the only `deepEqual`'d output remains a primitive:

```ts
const [allReports] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT);
const waitingReportIDs = Object.values(allReports ?? {})
    .filter((report): report is Report => !!report?.reportID && report.isWaitingOnBankAccount === true && report.ownerAccountID === accountID)
    .map((report) => report.reportID);
```

This subscribes to the entire `ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY` collection without a selector, but the only use of `policies` is to derive a single scalar (`getDefaultWorkspacePlanType(policies)` returns a `PolicyType`). Without a selector Onyx will re-render this page whenever *any* field of *any* policy changes, even though the page only cares about whether the user belongs to a Control workspace.

Use a selector that reduces the collection to the primitive the page actually needs, so `deepEqual` runs on a tiny value and unrelated policy updates don't trigger re-renders:

```tsx
const [defaultPlanType] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY, {
    selector: (policies) =>
        Object.values(policies ?? {}).some((policy) => policy?.type === CONST.POLICY.TYPE.CORPORATE)
            ? CONST.POLICY.TYPE.CORPORATE
            : CONST.POLICY.TYPE.TEAM,
});

const planType = workspaceConfirmationFormDraft?.planType ?? defaultPlanType;
```

This `useOnyx` subscribes to the entire `REPORT_ACTIONS` collection (one of the largest collections in the app) and its selector returns a `Record<string, boolean>` mapped across every policy report. Because a selector is used, Onyx runs `deepEqual` on this whole map on **every** report-action change anywhere in the app, and the selector also closes over the large external `policyReports` dataset and re-iterates it on each of those unrelated updates — compounding the cost. A mapped/transformed collection output like this is exactly the case PERF-11 warns against: the `deepEqual` is expensive and there is no meaningful re-render savings.

Instead, subscribe without a selector and build the lookup inline (or read presence per-report where it's needed), so Onyx uses the cheap `shallowEqual` on raw references:

```tsx
const [reportActions] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT_ACTIONS);

// where the room action is built:
const hasReportActions = !!reportActions?.[`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT_ACTIONS}${report.reportID}`];
openReport({reportID: report.reportID, introSelected, betas, shouldMarkAsRead: false, hasReportActions});
```

This avoids the per-report mapped object and the `deepEqual` over the full collection.

This `useOnyx` selector on `PERSONAL_DETAILS_LIST` returns `delegatorsPersonalDetailsList` (a full `PersonalDetailsList` of complete `PersonalDetails` objects) plus a full `delegatePersonalDetails` object. Because a selector is provided, Onyx runs `deepEqual` on this output on every change to the personal details collection. Returning full `PersonalDetails` objects makes that `deepEqual` expensive, and the selector also closes over `loginToAccountIDMap`, `account`, and `delegators`, iterating over `delegators` on every unrelated personal-details update — with no re-render savings, since the component only consumes `displayName`, `login`, `avatar`, and `accountID` (see `createBaseMenuItem`).

Narrow the selector output to only the fields the menu items need. This file already has `createDisplayDetailsByAccountIDsSelector` (returns `{accountID, displayName, login, avatar}` per account) for exactly this purpose — build the delegate + delegator accountID list and select just those display fields, e.g.:

```tsx
const accountIDs = [
    loginToAccountIDMap?.[account?.delegatedAccess?.delegate ?? ''],
    ...delegators.map((delegator) => loginToAccountIDMap?.[delegator.email]),
].filter((id): id is number => id !== undefined);
const [displayDetailsByAccountID] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.PERSONAL_DETAILS_LIST, {
    selector: createDisplayDetailsByAccountIDsSelector(accountIDs),
});
```

so `deepEqual` only compares the small picked fields instead of entire `PersonalDetails` records.

This new `personalDetailByLoginSelector` returns the entire `PersonalDetails` object, but its only consumer (`DelegateOnBehalfOfTextFallback`) uses just `detail?.displayName`. `PersonalDetails` is a large object (~20 fields, including nested `timezone`, `avatarStyle`, `status`, `errorFields`, and offline-feedback metadata). Because a `selector` is supplied, Onyx runs `deepEqual` on that full object on every change to the whole `PERSONAL_DETAILS_LIST` collection, and the component re-renders whenever any unrelated field of the matched user changes. The `find` over the collection to reduce to a single item is fine — the problem is returning the whole item instead of narrowing it.

Narrow the selector output to just what the consumer needs (mirroring the existing `createDisplayDetailsByAccountIDsSelector` pattern in this file), e.g.:

```ts
const personalDetailByLoginSelector =
    (login: string | undefined) =>
    (personalDetailsList: OnyxEntry<PersonalDetailsList>): string | undefined => {
        if (!login) {
            return undefined;
        }
        const lowerLogin = login.toLowerCase();
        return Object.values(personalDetailsList ?? {}).find((detail) => detail?.login?.toLowerCase() === lowerLogin)?.displayName;
    };
```

Returning the primitive `displayName` (or a small picked object if more fields are needed later) makes the `deepEqual` trivial and eliminates re-renders from unrelated field changes.

This subscribes to the entire `BANK_ACCOUNT_LIST` collection via `useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.BANK_ACCOUNT_LIST)`, but `bankAccountList` is only read inside `handleCtaPress` to look up a single account by `bankAccountID`. Without a selector, Onyx re-renders this widget whenever *any* bank account in the list changes, even ones unrelated to this item.

Narrow the subscription to just the account this widget cares about so unrelated updates don't trigger re-renders:

```tsx
const [accountData] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.BANK_ACCOUNT_LIST, {
    selector: (list) => list?.[String(bankAccountID)]?.accountData,
});
// ...
const additionalData = accountData?.additionalData;
```

The selector returns a small single-account object, so the `deepEqual` cost is negligible and re-renders are limited to changes to this account.

This `useOnyx` selector on `PERSONAL_DETAILS_LIST` returns an array of usernames whose size scales with the number of users on the private domain. With a selector, Onyx runs `deepEqual` on the selector output on every write to `PERSONAL_DETAILS_LIST`, so this is the documented anti-pattern of filtering/mapping a collection into an array — `deepEqual` walks every element on every unrelated update. The internal `memoize` only caches the scan; it does not remove the per-write `deepEqual` cost on the returned array.

Subscribe without a selector and transform inline (Onyx's `shallowEqual` on the raw reference is cheap), then compute the list in the hook body — the memoized `buildAvailableLoginsList` can still guarantee a stable reference so downstream consumers don't churn:

```tsx
const [personalDetails] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.PERSONAL_DETAILS_LIST);
const availableLoginsList = buildAvailableLoginsList(personalDetails, currentUserLogin);
```

`waitingReportsSelector` is a `useOnyx` selector over the entire `REPORT` collection that filters and maps it into an array of objects (`WaitingReportPaymentData[]`). Selectors that return a filtered/mapped array force Onyx to run `deepEqual` over every element on every collection update, with no re-render savings — and this array is only an intermediate structure that `shouldShowAddBankAccountSelector` further reduces to a boolean.

Subscribe to the collection without a selector and transform inline, or collapse the two selectors so the subscription computes the final boolean directly:

```tsx
const [allReports] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT);
const waitingReports = Object.values(allReports ?? {})
    .filter((report): report is Report => !!report?.reportID && report.isWaitingOnBankAccount === true && report.ownerAccountID === accountID)
    .map((report) => ({
        reportID: report.reportID,
        chatReportID: report.chatReportID,
        chatIOUReportID: report.chatReportID ? allReports?.[`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT}${report.chatReportID}`]?.iouReportID : undefined,
    }));
```

This newly added subscription reads the entire `REPORT_ACTIONS` collection with no selector. `REPORT_ACTIONS` holds actions for every report in the app, so this component will re-render on *any* report action change anywhere — even though `allReportActions` is only consumed inside the `handleMergeDuplicates`/`handleResolveDuplicates` callbacks at click time to look up a single report's actions by ID (`allReportActions?.[`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT_ACTIONS}${reportID}`]`). Subscribing to the whole collection reactively for an event-only read is an expensive, unnecessary render trigger.

Since the report IDs needed (`mergeParams.reportID`, etc.) are only known inside the callbacks, prefer reading the collection non-reactively at call time rather than subscribing to it in the render body. For example, use the module helper that already backs this data (e.g. `getAllReportActionsFromIOU()` / `getAllReportActions()`) inside the handlers, or pass the already-narrowed `reportActions` subscription (`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT_ACTIONS}${newTransaction?.reportID}`) that this component already has, so the component does not re-render on unrelated report-action updates:

```tsx
const handleMergeDuplicates = useCallback(() => {
    const allReportActions = getAllReportActions();
    mergeDuplicates({...mergeParams, ...taxData, currentUserAccountID, currentUserLogin: currentUserLogin ?? '', allTransactionViolations, allReportActionsList: allReportActions});
    // ...
}, [/* drop allReportActions from deps */]);
```

`getGroupPoliciesWhereReportCanBeCreated` returns `Object.values(policies).filter(...)`, i.e. an array of full `Policy` objects. Feeding this through a `useOnyx` `selector` means Onyx runs `deepEqual` over every policy object in that array on every `POLICY` collection update — this matches PERF-11's "Selector filters/maps a collection into an array — deepEqual on every item" anti-pattern, with no re-render savings to offset the cost.

Since this reshapes/filters a collection without reducing it to a small result, subscribe without a selector and derive the array inline so Onyx uses the cheap `shallowEqual`:

```tsx
const [allPolicies] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY);
const groupPoliciesWithChatEnabled = getGroupPoliciesWhereReportCanBeCreated(allPolicies, isSubmit2026BetaEnabled, email);
```

This `selector` filters the entire `POLICY_CATEGORIES` collection and rebuilds it with `Object.fromEntries(...)`, so its output is still a large collection-shaped object. Onyx runs `deepEqual` on that output on every update to the collection, and because the result is large the comparison is expensive with no re-render savings — exactly the pattern PERF-11 warns against ("Selector maps/filters an entire collection").

Prefer subscribing without a selector and transforming inline, so Onyx uses the cheap `shallowEqual` on raw references:

```tsx
const [rawPolicyCategories] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY_CATEGORIES);
const allPolicyCategories = Object.fromEntries(
    Object.entries(rawPolicyCategories ?? {}).filter(([key, categories]) => {
        if (key === `${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY_CATEGORIES}${personalPolicyID}`) {
            return false;
        }
        const availableCategories = Object.values(categories ?? {}).filter((category) => category.pendingAction !== CONST.RED_BRICK_ROAD_PENDING_ACTION.DELETE);
        return availableCategories.length > 0;
    }),
);
```

This hook subscribes to the full `SESSION` and `ACCOUNT` Onyx keys without selectors, so every consumer re-renders whenever *any* field on either object changes — even though the hook only needs `authTokenType`/`isSupportAuthTokenUsed` (session) and `delegatedAccess` (account). This is a regression from the code it replaces, which read those values through primitive-returning selectors (`authTokenTypeSelector`, `isActingAsDelegateSelector`). The hook is consumed in hot paths (ProductTrainingContext, TrackExpensePage, MultiScanContext, AuthScreens), so the extra re-renders are noticeable.

Narrow each subscription to the primitive(s) it needs so re-renders only fire when the derived boolean can actually change:

```tsx
const [isSupportalSession] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.SESSION, {selector: isSupportalSessionSelector});
const [isActingAsDelegate] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.ACCOUNT, {selector: isActingAsDelegateSelector});

return isSupportalSession || isActingAsDelegate;
```

This subscribes to the entire `ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT_NAME_VALUE_PAIRS` collection without a selector. Every consumer of this hook will re-render whenever any report's name-value-pairs change anywhere in the app, even though the value is ultimately only used by `isReportOutstanding` for the single current `report`.

Subscribe only to the report you need and pass a minimal single-entry collection to `getSecondaryReportActions`. For example, add a scoped subscription and build the collection from it:

```tsx
const [reportNameValuePairs] = useOnyx(`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT_NAME_VALUE_PAIRS}${getNonEmptyStringOnyxID(report?.reportID)}`);
// ...
const allReportNameValuePairs = report?.reportID
    ? {[`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT_NAME_VALUE_PAIRS}${report.reportID}`]: reportNameValuePairs}
    : undefined;
```

This avoids re-renders driven by unrelated report NVP updates.

This subscribes to the entire `ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT_NAME_VALUE_PAIRS` collection without a selector. Onyx will `shallowEqual` the raw collection reference, so this component now re-renders whenever *any* report's name-value-pairs change anywhere in the app, not just the one being displayed. Downstream, `allReportNameValuePairs` is only ever indexed for a single report inside `isReportOutstanding` (`reportNameValuePairs?.[`${PREFIX}${iouReport.reportID}`]`), and `iouReport` is the current `moneyRequestReport`.

This component already subscribes to the scoped single-report entry on line 115 (`reportNameValuePairs` keyed by `moneyRequestReport?.reportID`), which is exactly the entry the utility needs. Pass a narrow, single-entry collection instead of subscribing to the whole collection, e.g.:

```tsx
// remove the broad subscription and build a minimal collection from the scoped value
const allReportNameValuePairs = moneyRequestReport?.reportID
    ? {[`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT_NAME_VALUE_PAIRS}${moneyRequestReport.reportID}`]: reportNameValuePairs}
    : undefined;
```

