This `useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.PERSONAL_DETAILS_LIST, {selector: guidesEmailsByReportSelector})` subscription uses a selector that (1) returns a large `Record<string, boolean>` map with one entry per chat report, and (2) closes over the entire `chatReports` collection and iterates every report on each `PERSONAL_DETAILS_LIST` change. Per the rule, a selector that references another large external dataset via closure and iterates over it on every change to the subscribed key compounds cost, and its large output forces an expensive `deepEqual` on every update with no re-render savings. This is exactly the case the rule warns against.

Instead of computing a full report→boolean map inside a `PERSONAL_DETAILS_LIST` selector, subscribe to `PERSONAL_DETAILS_LIST` without a selector (cheap `shallowEqual`) and derive the per-report guides map inside a `useMemo` keyed on `chatReports` and the personal details, or compute `hasGuidesEmails` per report at the point of use (as the other call sites in this PR do with `hasExpensifyGuidesEmailsSelector(participantAccountIDs)`), so the expensive collection iteration doesn't run on every unrelated personal-details update.

```tsx
const [personalDetailsList] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.PERSONAL_DETAILS_LIST);
const guidesEmailsByReport = useMemo(() => {
    const map: Record<string, boolean> = {};
    for (const report of Object.values(chatReports ?? {})) {
        if (report) {
            const participantIDs = Object.keys(report.participants ?? {}).map(Number);
            map[report.reportID] = participantIDs.some((accountID) => Str.extractEmailDomain(personalDetailsList?.[accountID]?.login ?? '') === CONST.EMAIL.GUIDES_DOMAIN);
        }
    }
    return map;
}, [chatReports, personalDetailsList]);
```

