The `reportActionsSelector` returns full `ReportActions` objects (all actions for each relevant report), but the subsequent `useMemo` only extracts `childReportID` from the single matching IOU action per report. `ReportActions` objects can be very large, making `deepEqual` on the selector output expensive on every collection update.

Consider combining the selector logic with the final computation to return only the small result the hook actually needs. Since both `reportIDsKey` and `transactionIDsKey` are available, the selector could directly compute the `transactionID -> childReportID` mapping:

```ts
const reportActionsSelector = useCallback(
    (collection: OnyxCollection<ReportActions>) => {
        const rIds = reportIDsKey ? reportIDsKey.split('|') : [];
        const tIds = transactionIDsKey ? transactionIDsKey.split('|') : [];
        const result: Record<string, string | undefined> = {};
        for (const transactionID of tIds) {
            const tx = (transactions ?? []).find((t) => t?.transactionID === transactionID);
            if (!tx?.reportID) continue;
            const actions = collection?.[`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT_ACTIONS}${tx.reportID}`];
            const iouAction = Object.values(actions ?? {}).find((action) => {
                if (!isMoneyRequestAction(action)) return false;
                return getOriginalMessage(action)?.IOUTransactionID === transactionID;
            });
            result[transactionID] = iouAction?.childReportID;
        }
        return result;
    },
    [reportIDsKey, transactionIDsKey, transactions],
);
```

This returns a `Record<string, string | undefined>` (a few string values), making `deepEqual` nearly free and eliminating re-renders from unrelated report action changes.

The `transactionsSelector` returns full `Transaction` objects from the collection, but the hook only reads `reportID` and `transactionID` from each transaction (lines 59-60). Returning full `Transaction` objects means Onyx runs `deepEqual` on the entire array of transaction objects on every collection update, which is expensive when any unrelated field on those transactions changes.

