`selectSnapshotReportIDs` iterates over all keys in the snapshot data and returns a `string[]` of report IDs. This array can be large, and `useOnyx` will run `deepEqual` on it after every snapshot update -- matching the rule's explicit bad example: "Selector filters/maps a collection into an array -- deepEqual on every item."

Since the outer component only checks `snapshotReportIDs.length > 1 && snapshotReportIDs.includes(reportID)`, consider computing the final result inside the selector to return a small object or primitives instead of the full array. For example, pass `reportID` and compute the guard condition directly:

```tsx
// Return a small object instead of a large array
const selectSnapshotGuard = (snapshot: OnyxEntry<SearchResults>, reportID: string): {hasMultiple: boolean; includesReport: boolean} => {
    const data = snapshot?.data;
    if (!data) return {hasMultiple: false, includesReport: false};
    let count = 0;
    let includesReport = false;
    for (const key of Object.keys(data)) {
        if (key.startsWith(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT)) {
            count++;
            if (key.slice(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT.length) === reportID) includesReport = true;
            if (count > 1 && includesReport) break; // early exit
        }
    }
    return {hasMultiple: count > 1, includesReport};
};
```

This reduces `deepEqual` cost from O(n) array comparison to O(1) on two booleans.

Also note: the `!key.startsWith(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT_ACTIONS)` and `!key.startsWith(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT_NAME_VALUE_PAIRS)` checks are redundant since `'reportActions_'` and `'reportNameValuePairs_'` do not start with `'report_'`.

